{
  "image_name": "e7c0ef0c2df90027c9ce99031186431a.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a class of redox-flow-style batteries that use a solid electrolyte to separate two liquid electrolytes while allowing only small “mediator” ions to pass between them. In the top schematic, an anolyte and catholyte are split by a mediator-ion solid-state electrolyte, with current collectors at both ends. During charging and discharging, electrons travel through the external circuit, while mediator cations shuttle through the solid separator. Each liquid side contains redox-active species that are oxidized or reduced at the electrodes, showing how charge storage can be decoupled from ion transport.\n\nThe lower panels give representative chemistries built on this concept. One example pairs a zinc anode chemistry in alkaline solution, involving Zn, ZnO, OH−, and water, with a bromine catholyte where bromide forms Br2, while Li+ crosses a Li-conducting solid electrolyte. Another combines iron chemistry on the anode side, apparently Fe/Fe(OH)2 in alkaline media, with a ferri/ferrocyanide-type catholyte across a Na-conducting solid electrolyte. A further example links the zinc alkaline side to an acidic oxygen-evolution catholyte involving phosphate species and O2. Overall, the figure emphasizes modular pairing of different anode and cathode chemistries through selective ion-conducting solid separators.",
